/*
Problem: Search in a Binary Search Tree (LC 700)
Topic: Binary Search Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

A Binary Search Tree (BST) has the
property:

• Left subtree values < root value

• Right subtree values > root value

Use this property to eliminate half
of the tree at every step.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. If the current node is NULL,
   return NULL.

2. If the current node's value equals
   the target, return the node.

3. If the target is smaller than the
   current value, search the left
   subtree.

4. Otherwise, search the right
   subtree.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

        4
       / \
      2   7
     / \
    1   3

Target = 2

Search path:

4 → 2

Answer:

Node with value 2

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

        4
       / \
      2   7
     / \
    1   3

Target = 5

Search path:

4 → 7 → NULL

Answer:

NULL

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

At every node, the BST property tells
us exactly which subtree may contain
the target.

The other subtree can be ignored,
making the search much faster than a
normal binary tree search.

--------------------------------------------------

Time :

Average : O(log n)

Balanced BST.

Worst : O(n)

Skewed BST.

Space :

Average : O(log n)

Worst : O(n)

Due to recursion stack.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Binary Search Tree / Recursion
*/

class Solution {
public:

    TreeNode* searchBST(TreeNode* root, int val) {

        // Base case
        if (root == NULL)
            return NULL;

        // Target found
        if (root->val == val)
            return root;

        // Search left subtree
        if (val < root->val)
            return searchBST(root->left, val);

        // Search right subtree
        return searchBST(root->right, val);
    }
};
